    {
      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the area of the square, we first need to determine the length of its edge. We are given two expressions for the edge length: \\(4x - 15\\) meters and \\(20 - 3x\\) meters. Since both expressions represent the same length, we can set them equal to each other and solve for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n4x - 15 = 20 - 3x\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(x\\), we first add \\(3x\\) to both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\n4x + 3x - 15 = 20\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n7x - 15 = 20\n\\]\n\nNext, we add 15 to both sides to further isolate the term with \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n7x = 35\n\\]\n\nNow, we divide both sides by 7 to solve for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx = 5\n\\]\n\nWith \\(x = 5\\), we can substitute this value back into either expression for the edge length to find the actual length of the edge. Using \\(4x - 15\\):\n\n\\[\n4(5) - 15 = 20 - 15 = 5 \\text{ meters}\n\\]\n\nSo, the length of each edge of the square is 5 meters. The area \\(A\\) of a square is given by the square of its edge length:\n\n\\[\nA = 5^2 = 25 \\text{ square meters}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the square is \\(\\boxed{25}\\).",
