      "text": "1. Given that the edge of the square can be expressed as \\(4x - 15\\) meters or as \\(20 - 3x\\) meters, we start by setting these two expressions equal to each other to find the value of \\(x\\):\n   \\[\n   4x - 15 = 20 - 3x\n   \\]\n\n2. To solve for \\(x\\), we first add \\(3x\\) to both sides of the equation:\n   \\[\n   4x + 3x - 15 = 20\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   7x - 15 = 20\n   \\]\n\n3. Next, we add 15 to both sides to isolate the term with \\(x\\):\n   \\[\n   7x - 15 + 15 = 20 + 15\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   7x = 35\n   \\]\n\n4. Finally, we divide both sides by 7 to solve for \\(x\\):\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{35}{7} = 5\n   \\]\n\n5. Now that we have the value of \\(x\\), we substitute it back into one of the expressions for the edge length of the square. Using \\(4x - 15\\):\n   \\[\n   4(5) - 15 = 20 - 15 = 5 \\text{ meters}\n   \\]\n\n6. The edge length of the square is 5 meters. The area \\(A\\) of a square is given by the square of its edge length:\n   \\[\n   A = 5^2 = 25 \\text{ square meters}\n   \\]\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{25}\n\\]",
